I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern format, and the scientific tooling available in this workspace, then identify candidate phases and refine them jointly in GSAS-II. I’ll keep phase fractions normalized to the modeled crystalline content and flag any ambiguity in the final report.

The chemistry strongly suggests calcium tungstates, but I won’t assume a single scheelite phase: I’m first extracting peak positions and checking whether every reflection can be indexed by CaWO₄ or whether residual/reacted secondary phases are required.

Peak positions and relative intensities match tetragonal scheelite CaWO₄ (space group I4₁/a), including the nearby 28.70° (112) and 29.03° (103) reflections; those are not two phases. No diagnostic WO₃, CaO, or Ca₃WO₆ peaks are evident. I’m now building the quantitative GSAS-II model and testing whether a second crystalline phase improves unsupported residual features.

The initial GSAS-II model uses the reported Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ doublet and reflection geometry. I’m refining in stages—background/scale/cell, profile, specimen shift, then only the symmetry-allowed oxygen coordinates and isotropic displacement factors—to expose instability rather than hide it in a large simultaneous fit.

The first profile refinement already reduced Rwp from 14.7% to 4.4%, confirming the phase assignment. One unconstrained Lorentzian width term moved negative, so I’m tightening the physically allowed profile parameterization before trusting the later structural steps.

The constrained structural fit reaches Rwp ≈3.10% (close to the 2.28% counting-statistics floor), with all Bragg intensity accounted for. Two nuisance/thermal terms hit imposed bounds, so I’m checking those boundary effects now; I will not report boundary values as physically meaningful merely because they lower Rwp.

A deliberately over-complete trial model puts only about 0.15–0.17 wt% into CaO/WO₃ and drives Ca₃WO₆ to zero, but the joint trial became numerically unstable at the nonnegative boundary. I’m rerunning each possible impurity independently and checking whether its diagnostic peaks—not just a tiny R-factor change—are reproducible above counting noise.

The independent impurity tests support a one-phase submission: Ca₃WO₆ refines to zero; CaO changes χ² by only ~6; and a nominal ~0.15 wt% WO₃ term gives a small statistical improvement but fails the strongest three-peak coherence test. I’m treating that as a possible sub-quantification trace, not a reliably identified second phase, and finalizing the physically constrained one-phase project.

The required deliverables are now written. I’m doing a final consistency pass: reopening the GSAS-II project, parsing the exported CIF/JSON, and verifying that every row in `fit.csv` exactly matches the final project’s observed and calculated arrays.

Completed. The pattern is reliably single-phase scheelite CaWO₄ (I4₁/a), 100.00 wt% of modeled crystalline phases. Final fit: Rwp 2.802%, Rp 2.133%, GOF 1.228. A possible sub-0.2 wt% WO₃ trace was below reliable identification/quantification.

